  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through two innings before Grisham's homer in the third. Then they held on from there.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole gave Minnesota nothing after the second, striking out seven across five innings on a clean 5-hit line. The bullpen took the final four frames without breaking: Headrick and Blackburn each threw a scoreless inning with two strikeouts apiece, then Cruz and Bednar closed it out with zero damage and three more Ks in the ninth. The offense didn't need to be perfect. Grisham delivered the one swing that mattered. A two-run shot in the third that flipped the game from trailing to leading. And the back end held the rest. Two runs in the seventh added breathing room the starter had already earned. This is what a 5-2 win looks like when everything aligns: pitching that dominates, relief that doesn't waver, and one clear moment where the bats converted runners on base into runs instead of leaving them stranded. The margin held because it was built early and protected late. Nothing about this felt fragile. They were never behind after the third.",
    "what_to_watch": "Minnesota starts Zebby Matthews tonight (4.15 ERA), and Brendan Beck takes the mound for the Yankees. Beck hasn't pitched yet this season, so the opportunity is there. But Matthews has been consistent enough in the Twins' rotation that the Yankees won't get a free inning.",
